      "problem": "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{3}t-2 \\\\ y=5-4t \\\\ z=2t+\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line.",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}t-2 \\\\ 5-4t \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}t \\\\ -4t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-2,5,\\frac{1}{7})$  and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$",
