  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-71035",
      "knowledge_point_id": "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Line When a Component of the Direction Vector is Zero",
      "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6+10t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{y+6}{10}=\\frac{z-2}{3},\\,x=3",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y-10}{6}=\\frac{z-3}{-2}",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y+10}{6}=\\frac{z+3}{-2}",
              "option": "3",
